But maybe we should keep Q6 as one group to avoid over-splitting. Which is more aligned with "smallest set that preserves useful task structure without collapsing independent objectives"? The last bullet has two objectives: number of tables, and emotion percentage. They are independent. So splitting is justified. It results in 6 groups. That seems good.

Let's check requirement 4: "Do not group independent requirements merely because they contribute to the same final answer." So splitting Q6 is correct.

What about Q4? "Two people wrote it. One has another research paper..." This contains two requirements? It says two people wrote it. And one has another paper. The second is an attribute of one of the two. They are related but could be independent? The paper has two authors; one of those authors has another paper. The second depends on the first. If we split, we'd have "Two people wrote it." and "One has another research paper in the Journal of Baltic Science Education written between 2016 and 2023." The second span "One has..." is incomplete because "One" refers to one of the two people. So grouping is necessary to preserve relation. So keep Q4 as one group.

What about Q5? It says "one of the writers" which refers to Q4's writers. It is a separate attribute. Could be grouped with Q4? If grouped, it would be one group about authors: two people wrote it, one has another paper, and one was academic staff. But that's multiple independent facts about authors. They are different attributes. So separate.

